  ID; on success the handler calls [38;5;153mIdentity.setUserId(result.userId)[39m. Every subsequent login and every later form gets
  a real ID from [38;5;153mgetUserId()[39m.

  This requires one change to the stub: it currently returns [38;5;153m{ success: true, user: username }[39m with no ID, so the
  persistence path would never fire and you couldn't see the feature work. I'll have it return a clearly-marked stub ID,
  with a comment that the real [38;5;153mPOST[39m to [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m supplies the authoritative value. That stub line gets deleted when
  the real call lands.

  [1m3. Security and privacy posture[0m

  This is the section I flagged earlier; it's the one with consequences past the lines we touch.

  - [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m is origin-scoped and readable by [1many[0m script on the origin. The ID is not a secret, but it is an
    identifier — an XSS on this page can read it. Acceptable for a non-secret tracking ID; not acceptable if this ever
    holds a session token. I'd note that constraint in a comment so nobody upgrades this into a token store.
  - [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m is [1mper-tab[0m. Two tabs means two independent IDs until each logs in. Correct, but surprising if you
    later reason about it as one-ID-per-user.
  - [38;5;153mclearUserId()[39m will have no caller — there's no logout in this app. It's the documented clearing contract for when
    one exists. Say the word and I'll drop it until then.
  - The password is never logged today and won't be.

  [1m4. Error handling[0m

  [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m throws in some privacy modes rather than returning null. All three functions wrap access in [38;5;153mtry[39m/[38;5;153mcatch[39m:
  [38;5;153mgetUserId[39m degrades to [38;5;153mnull[39m, [38;5;153msetUserId[39m becomes a no-op after one [38;5;153mconsole.warn[39m. Login must keep working when storage is
  unavailable — tracking is the thing that degrades, never authentication. A failed login stores nothing.

  [1m5. Testing and tooling[0m

  This project has no tooling at all: [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m has no [38;5;153mscripts[39m and no [38;5;153mdevDependencies[39m, and there are no tests. The
  identity module has genuinely testable logic — null handling, the storage-throws path, clear-after-set — and it's
  cheapest to set this up now, before the code exists.

